0010-pace-redraws-on-display-link.patch

Paint on the display's refresh instead of on a free-running 16.67ms timer.

Upstream coalesces redraws with DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(+16.67ms) from the
first queued byte (queuePendingDisplay, queueMetalDisplay). That timer has no
relationship to vsync, so:

  * a steady 60fps producer (agent-afk's ink reveal through tmux) is coalesced to
    ~30fps: the timer is still pending when every second update arrives, so every
    other frame is dropped (measured 94/188 painted, p50 interval ~32ms at 120Hz);
  * the paints that do happen land at an arbitrary, drifting phase inside the
    refresh period (~2ms spread at 120Hz), so the compositor shows some one
    refresh late and some on time, which reads as uneven motion.

Three changes:

1. Mac/MacDisplayLinkPacer.swift (new) — TerminalDisplayPacer, driven by
   NSView.displayLink(target:selector:) (macOS 14+) in .common run-loop mode.
   Paints only on a tick with something pending; pauses after 8 idle ticks;
   a 100ms watchdog paints a frame the link failed to deliver (view ordered out,
   minimised); rebinds on window change so the link follows the screen's rate.
   SWIFTTERM_DISPLAY_LINK=0 (or TerminalView.displayLinkPacingEnabled = false)
   restores upstream's timer verbatim. TerminalView.frameTimingObserver is called
   once per real paint so pacing can be measured.

2. Apple/AppleTerminalView.swift — queuePendingDisplay and queueMetalDisplay try
   the pacer first and fall through to upstream's asyncAfter when it declines
   (off-main, pre-14, no window, kill switch). updateDisplay calls the timing
   observer after clearUpdateRange. The DEC 2026 synchronizedOutputActive checks
   are unchanged, so synchronized output is honoured by the same code as before.

3. Mac/MacTerminalView.swift — the displayPacer property, a windowDidChange()
   call in viewDidMoveToWindow, and invalidate() in deinit.

Gate: app/Scripts/check-display-link.sh (both renderers; its case 8 requires the
old timer to FAIL the same bounds).

